  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nThrough Thursday: Local radars showed the last of the rain \nassociated with a cold front moving through our CWA, just to the \nsoutheast of our CWA. A surface high to the northwest will settle \nover our region tonight into Thursday. This will bring in drier air \nin the wake of the cold front and allow temperatures to bottom in \nthe 40s at most locations. Under strong radiational cooling tonight, \npatchy fog will be possible toward sunrise. The near zonal flow \naloft tonight will become southwesterly Thursday. Combined with the \nsurface high and near full insolation, temperatures will warm above \nnormal, peaking near 80F in the southeast. /22/ \n\nThrough early next week...\n\nWhile the pattern will be substantially more suppressed heading\ninto the weekend with high pressure and continental air largely in\ncontrol, an active southern stream jet will provide for an\nopportunity for increased cloudiness and perhaps an isolated\nshower or two through early next week. Overall, though, most\nlocations will stay dry and those that do see a brief shower won't\nsee much. The next 7 days will likely be dry and cool as overnight\nlows average 5 to 10 F below climate normals for this time of year.\n\nLows temperatures at times will flirt with the mid to lower 30s F,\nparticularly in some of the typically cooler spots. This could \nresult in some chance for a light frost though most places likely\nmanage to stay above freezing. The coldest night appears to come\nSunday night before we begin a slow moderating trend (lows \"only\"\ndown into the lower 40s F) into the middle of next week. \nRegardless, these near-freezing temperatures serve to remind that\nnow is a good time to begin practicing those winter safety \npractices. Ensuring plants, pets, and people are considered for as\nnights begin to more routinely threaten frost/freeze. /LP/KP",
